## Summary

If the LMCache engine's shutdown is not called, LMCache's background threads keep the host process alive. The process then cannot exit until it is killed.

## Details

LMCache starts several long-running background threads when it is used as a vLLM KV connector:
- the lookup server and offload server threads (ZMQ receive loops);
- the internal API server threads (asyncio event loops);
- the storage manager's event loop thread;
- the stats logger and pin monitor threads.

These threads run until LMCache's shutdown stops them, and at least some of them are non-daemon. When shutdown is never called, Python's interpreter exit (`threading._shutdown`) waits for them indefinitely.

This happens today with vLLM's built-in `LMCacheConnectorV1`, which does not forward `shutdown()` (vllm-project/vllm#62808). On SIGTERM, vLLM's EngineCore finishes its busy loop and then hangs at interpreter exit. vLLM force-kills it at the end of its shutdown timeout.

A Python stack dump taken during that wait shows:
- the main thread in `threading._shutdown`;
- LMCache's lookup-server, offload-server, api-server, storage-manager event-loop, stats-logger and pin-monitor threads all still alive.

## Expected

An embedded library should not be able to block its host process from exiting because one shutdown call was missed. Options:
- make the background threads daemon threads, if they hold nothing that must be flushed on exit;
- or stop them from an interpreter-exit hook that runs before non-daemon threads are joined;
- and log a warning when the process exits without LMCache having been shut down.

## Environment

LMCache current dev, vLLM 0.31.0, `kv_role: kv_both`, local-disk backend, single node, TP=1.

ModelWarmup is available as an experimental feature in AIBrix v0.8.0. We would like more users and cluster operators to try it and share what works, what is unclear, and what fails in real environments.

ModelWarmup prepares selected Kubernetes nodes before workload placement. It can:

- preload container images;
- run finite custom preparation actions, including model downloads and node checks;
- run once against a resolved set of nodes;
- continuously cover nodes as they join, leave, or are replaced.

ModelWarmup creates a node-pinned Job for each selected node. It prepares caches only. It does not start an inference server or gate workload placement.

The feature is experimental in v0.8.0, so its API and behavior may change based on feedback.

References:

- [AIBrix v0.8.0 release](https://github.com/vllm-project/aibrix/releases/tag/v0.8.0)
- [ModelWarmup documentation](https://github.com/vllm-project/aibrix/blob/v0.8.0/docs/source/features/model-serving/model-warmup.rst)
- [ModelWarmup samples](https://github.com/vllm-project/aibrix/tree/v0.8.0/samples/modelwarmup)

## Start with a small smoke test

After installing AIBrix v0.8.0, authorize a test namespace and node in the same resource pool:

```bash
kubectl label namespace default resource-pool.aibrix.ai/name=latency-pool
kubectl label node <node> \
  resource-pool.aibrix.ai/name=latency-pool \
  model.aibrix.ai/warmup-enabled=true
```

Apply the lightweight image-only sample:

```bash
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/vllm-project/aibrix/v0.8.0/samples/modelwarmup/modelwarmup.yaml
kubectl get modelwarmup runtime-image-warmup -w
```

The sample pulls `busybox:1.36` on each authorized target node and exits. A successful run reaches the `Succeeded` phase.

## What we would like tested

We are interested in feedback from different Kubernetes distributions, container runtimes, registries, storage configurations, and GPU environments.

Useful test areas include:

- image preloading with public and private registries;
- custom model downloads and node-local cache permissions;
- node and GPU prechecks;
- the combined vLLM and SGLang samples;
- `Once` behavior with multiple nodes and partial failures;
- `Continuous` behavior when nodes join, leave, are relabeled, or are replaced;
- retries, timeouts, and controller recovery;
- taints, Pod Security admission, quotas, and restricted namespaces;
- status, events, diagnostics, and documentation clarity.

Successful test reports are useful too. Please include the environment and sample you tried so we can understand where ModelWarmup is already working.

## Reporting a problem

For a bug, open a separate issue and link back to this one. Please include:

- AIBrix version or commit;
- Kubernetes version and container runtime;
- ModelWarmup manifest with credentials removed;
- expected and actual behavior;
- `kubectl describe modelwarmup <name>`;
- generated Job and Pod status or events;
- relevant controller logs.

## Contributing

Pull requests are welcome for bug fixes, tests, documentation, diagnostics, and sample improvements. Please link the PR to the reported problem and describe how you tested the change.

For API changes or larger behavior changes, start with an issue or discussion before opening a large PR. This gives users and maintainers time to agree on the requirement while the feature is still experimental.

